      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2001 S. Karger AG, Basel",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Increasing evidence regarding asthma suggests that CD4+ cells are preferentially recruited to sites of bronchial inflammation. Interleukin (IL)-16 has been reported as playing an important role in the accumulation of CD4+ cells. We have shown that the CD4 molecule is expressed on normal human eosinophils by tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha stimulation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We evaluated the effects of theophylline, KF19514 [a selective phosphodiesterase (PDE) IV inhibitor] and dexamethasone on CD4 expression on eosinophils and eosinophil migration in response to IL-16, a natural soluble ligand of the CD4 molecule."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The maximum eosinophil migration was observed when eosinophils were cultured with TNF-alpha at 10 ng/ml for 18 h and the concentration of IL-16 was 10 pg/ml. CD4+ eosinophil migration in response to IL-16 was mostly, if not fully, chemokinetic and this migration was significantly inhibited by Fab of anti-CD4 monoclonal antibody. Theophylline (10(-4)-10(-3) M), KF19514 (10(-7)-10(-6) M) and dexamethasone (10(-8)- 10(-6) M) significantly inhibited CD4 expression on eosinophils induced by TNF-alpha. Theophylline (10(-3) M) and KF19514 (10(-6) M) inhibited CD4+ eosinophil migratory responses induced by IL-16, but 10(-6) M dexamethasone did not. Theophylline and KF19514 augmented the intracellular adenosine-3',5'-cyclic monophosphate (cAMP) concentration in eosinophils, suggesting modulation by cAMP of CD4 expression and eosinophil migration."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These data suggest that TNF-alpha-induced CD4+ eosinophils may contribute to eosinophil migratory responses induced by IL-16. Theophylline and selective PDE IV inhibitor may prevent airway inflammation by downregulating CD4 expression on eosinophils and inhibiting eosinophil migration through CD4 and IL-16 interaction."
          }
        ]
      },
